### trying to escape a quote inside single quotes
echo 'It\''s late'
>>> SC1003 1:9

### value ending in an escaped quote
greeting='hello world\'
echo "$greeting"
>>> SC1003 1:22

### backslash followed by a letter in single quotes
echo 'C:\Users\guest'
>>> none

### backslash followed by a digit in single quotes
sed 's/\(x\)/\1/' input.txt
>>> none

### strings of only backslashes or ending in an escaped backslash
tr '\' '/' < input.txt
printf '\e]0;%s\e\\' title
json='\\'
echo "$json"
>>> none
>>- SC1003 1:5 -- a string holding only a backslash, as in tr '\' '/', is that backslash; there is no text whose quote it could be escaping
>>- SC1003 2:19 -- an even run of backslashes is a doubled backslash for printf, sed or JSON, not an attempt to escape the quote
>>- SC1003 3:8 -- an even run of backslashes is a doubled backslash for printf, sed or JSON, not an attempt to escape the quote
